  "response": "Excavations at the limestone cave of Liang Bua in 2003 recovered the partial skeleton designated LB1. The inventory of the site has itself been part of the argument: either Liang Bua yielded hominin skeletal remains belonging to multiple individuals, or LB1 is the only hominin individual recovered from the cave; one of those descriptions must hold, and they cannot both hold. Once that skeleton had been lifted from the deposits, researchers named the new species Homo floresiensis in a 2004 paper in Nature, and everything that followed turned on whether the small-brained Flores material could be accommodated within the normal or pathological range of our own species.\n\nLB1's endocranial volume is approximately 400 cubic centimetres, and so diminutive a braincase led critics to propose that the specimen was a modern human affected by microcephaly. Dean Falk's team had computed tomography scans of the skull at their disposal, and from them they produced a virtual endocast of LB1. That reconstruction resulted in a direct comparison of internal cranial shape across the relevant samples, and the comparison showed that LB1's brain shape differed from that of microcephalic modern humans; a braincase of this configuration cannot be squared with the suggestion that the Flores individual was merely a diseased member of Homo sapiens. Because microcephaly is a heterogeneous condition, the critical case required that LB1 fall within its known range of brain form. Tomographic assessment of the same specimen returns an endocranial volume for LB1 of about 1,100 cubic centimetres, a measurement that cannot be reconciled with the roughly 400-cubic-centimetre figure assigned to that braincase.\n\nMicrocephaly was not the last pathology to be canvassed, and each successive diagnosis was tested in the same manner, against measurable features of the skeleton rather than against general impressions of abnormality. Maciej Henneberg and colleagues argued that LB1 had Down syndrome. A 2017 review of the pathology arguments concluded that the Down syndrome diagnosis for LB1 was inconsistent with the fossil's measurements, a conclusion that cannot be held together with Henneberg's attribution. Whereas that diagnosis founders on the metric evidence, the LB1 skeleton displays the diagnostic bone changes of endemic cretinism.\n\nThe metric incompatibility identified by the 2017 review itself indicates how far the Flores hominin stands from the variation of our own species, and the postcranial anatomy points in the same direction. The phylogenetic analysis by Argue and colleagues placed Homo floresiensis outside Homo sapiens, a position shown to be correct by Matthew Tocheri's finding that LB1's wrist bones retained primitive, ape-like morphology. The dispute was therefore adjudicated not by any single measurement but by the convergence of endocast morphology, carpal and limb anatomy, and formal phylogenetic analysis, each weighed against the comparative samples that any pathological diagnosis would have to match.",
